[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"MVVM vs MVI Architecture Patterns in Android (Kotlin)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right architecture pattern is crucial for building scalable, maintainable, and testable Android applications. Two of the most prominent reactive architecture patterns in modern Android development are MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) and MVI (Model-View-Intent). In this tutorial, we will explore both patterns, compare their implementations using Kotlin, and understand when to use each."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVVM decouples the UI (Activities, Fragments, or Composables) from the business logic. The ViewModel holds the state and exposes it to the View via observable data holders like StateFlow or LiveData. The View observes this state and updates itself accordingly."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Model: Represents the data and business logic (Repositories, DataSources)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"View: Observes the ViewModel and renders the UI."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ViewModel: Manages UI-related data and survives configuration changes."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class UserViewModel : ViewModel() {
    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow<UserUiState>(UserUiState.Loading)
    val uiState: StateFlow<UserUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()

    fun loadUser(userId: String) {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            _uiState.value = UserUiState.Loading
            try {
                val user = userRepository.fetchUser(userId)
                _uiState.value = UserUiState.Success(user)
            } catch (e: Exception) {
                _uiState.value = UserUiState.Error(e.localizedMessage ?: "Unknown error")
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding MVI (Model-View-Intent)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVI is built on three core principles: State, Intent, and Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF). Unlike MVVM where multiple disparate functions can mutate state independently, MVI funnels all user actions into Intents. These Intents are reduced into a single, immutable ViewState."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Intent: Represents an user action or event that triggers a state change."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Model: Represents the immutable UI state."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Source of Truth: State flows in one direction only (Intent -> ViewModel -> State -> View)."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"sealed interface UserIntent {
    data class LoadUser(val userId: String) : UserIntent
}

sealed interface UserState {
    object Loading : UserState
    data class Success(val user: User) : UserState
    data class Error(val message: String) : UserState
}

class UserMviViewModel : ViewModel() {
    private val _state = MutableStateFlow<UserState>(UserState.Loading)
    val state: StateFlow<UserState> = _state.asStateFlow()

    fun handleIntent(intent: UserIntent) {
        when (intent) {
            is UserIntent.LoadUser -> fetchUser(intent.userId)
        }
    }

    private fun fetchUser(userId: String) {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            _state.value = UserState.Loading
            try {
                val user = userRepository.fetchUser(userId)
                _state.value = UserState.Success(user)
            } catch (e: Exception) {
                _state.value = UserState.Error(e.localizedMessage ?: "Unknown error")
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences and When to Use"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"State Management: MVVM allows multiple mutable variables or separate flows. MVI enforces a single, immutable state object containing all screen data."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Complexity: MVVM is easier to learn and requires less boilerplate code. MVI introduces a steeper learning curve with Intents and Reducers."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Testability: MVI excels in testability because state transitions are deterministic and predictable based on emitted intents."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use MVVM for standard CRUD applications, simpler UIs, and teams transitioning from traditional Android development. Use MVI for complex applications with numerous edge cases, heavy concurrent user interactions, and where strict predictability and time-travel debugging are required."}]
